# EmptySock Engine — Cursor / Windsurf Rules

You are working on a game built with EmptySock (@emptysock/engine).
TypeScript preferred, JavaScript fully supported. bitECS-backed entity/component core, PixiJS rendering, Rapier2D/3D physics, Howler.js audio. Tauri v2 desktop shell.

## Absolute rules

NEVER import pixi.js, @dimforge/rapier2d/3d, or howler directly.
NEVER use `any` type. Use `unknown` + Zod or type guards.
NEVER use `!` non-null assertions. Use `?.` and `?? fallback`.
NEVER use setTimeout or setInterval in game logic. Use the engine's own Timer/TweenManager.
NEVER write `async onUpdate()` — it's a type error in TS (onUpdate is `(dt: number) => void`) and a loud runtime warning in JS. Use entity.startCoroutine() for multi-frame work.
NEVER access the DOM directly in game/scene code.
ALWAYS call scene.destroy(entity) when an entity is no longer needed — this is the same call whether or not the entity was pooled.
ALWAYS parse external/saved JSON through a Zod schema or explicit guard before use.
ALWAYS add explicit return types to functions.
ALWAYS commit with conventional format: feat/fix/refactor/chore(scope): message

## Core API — quick reference

new Game() then game.loadScene(sceneDefinition) — Game creates/destroys that scene's ActorSystem/PhysicsSystem for you.

defineScene({ onLoad(scene, {actors, physics, input, audio}), onUpdate(dt), onUnload(scene) })

Entities are handles, not classes:
  entity = scene.spawn('name')          — or scene.spawn(somePrefab, props, { pool?: true })
  entity.add(ComponentDef, overrides?)  → live component data
  entity.get(ComponentDef)              → T | undefined
  entity.has(ComponentDef)              → boolean
  entity.isAlive                        → boolean (see pooling note below)
  scene.destroy(entity)
  scene.each(CompA, CompB, (a, b, entity) => {...})   — bulk path, skips per-entity proxy overhead

defineComponent(name, () => defaults, { version?, schema? }) — name is load-bearing across hot-reload.
Component fields must be JSON-serializable — no functions, no class instances. A callback-shaped
property (like PhysicsBody's collision handlers) lives in a side-table, assigned like a normal field.

Pooling: scene.spawn(prefab, props, { pool: true }); scene.destroy(entity) recycles it.
Gotcha: a pooled-and-destroyed entity's isAlive stays true — check .has()/.get() instead, not isAlive.

Physics: player.get(PhysicsBody).onCollisionEnter = (other, contact) => {...} — assigning IS registering.
Bodies collide with everything by default; collision groups are opt-in.

Saving: new SaveSystem(scene, [ComponentDef, ...]); await save.save('slot'); await save.load('slot').
Version-bump a component (defineComponent(name, defaults, {version: 2})) + save.registerMigration(name, fn)
when its shape changes. No migration registered → that one component's data is dropped with a warning,
not the whole save.

Input (InputManager, game.input): snapshot is frozen for the whole frame — isDown()/keyboard/gamepad()/
touches all read that frozen copy. game.input.attach() is called by your bootstrap code, never by Game itself.

Optional module packages (never auto-imported by the core engine):
  @emptysock/network  — Colyseus multiplayer, networked(componentDef, ['field', ...])
  @emptysock/vn       — VNSystem / Story Graph
  @emptysock/battle   — BattleSystem, turn-based combat
  @emptysock/tilemap  — TilemapSystem, NavMeshSystem

## Commit examples

feat(player): add wall-jump mechanic
fix(save): migrate Inventory component to its new field shape
refactor(enemy): extract patrol logic into PatrolComponent

